CAT = "cparegEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("A CPA preparing a client's current return learns that the client left income off a prior-year return. Under Circular 230, the CPA must:",
 "Advise the client of the omission", ["Notify the IRS of the omission", "Amend the prior return at once", "Withdraw from the engagement"],
 "Circular 230 section 10.21 requires the practitioner to promptly advise the client of the noncompliance and its consequences. The practitioner has no duty to tell the IRS or to amend the return without the client."),
("Under Circular 230, a practitioner may charge a contingent fee for:",
 "Representing a client in Tax Court", ["Preparing an original tax return", "Planning a client's estimated tax", "Advising on a business purchase"],
 "Section 10.27 allows contingent fees for IRS audits or challenges of an original return, certain refund claims and judicial proceedings. Fees for preparing an original return may not be contingent."),
("A practitioner has a conflict of interest between two clients. Under Circular 230, the practitioner may still represent both if each client:",
 "Gives informed written consent", ["Gives oral consent in advance", "Is told after the work ends", "Pays a separate retainer"],
 "Section 10.29 permits representation despite a conflict when the practitioner reasonably believes they can represent each client competently, the law allows it, and each affected client gives informed consent confirmed in writing."),
("A client in a fee dispute asks for the original records needed to file their return. Under Circular 230, the practitioner must generally:",
 "Return the client's records", ["Keep them until paid in full", "Send them straight to the IRS", "Return only photocopies"],
 "Section 10.28 requires a practitioner to promptly return a client's records needed for the client to meet federal tax obligations, even in a fee dispute. The practitioner may keep copies."),
("Under Circular 230, a practitioner may generally rely in good faith on information a client provides unless it:",
 "Appears incorrect or incomplete", ["Was not signed by a notary", "Was given to them orally", "Lacks third-party proof"],
 "A practitioner exercises due diligence but may rely on client information without verifying it. If the information looks wrong, inconsistent or incomplete, the practitioner must make reasonable inquiries."),
("Who is a tax return preparer under the Internal Revenue Code?",
 "A person paid to prepare a return", ["Only a CPA or enrolled agent", "A spouse preparing a joint return", "A volunteer at a free tax site"],
 "A tax return preparer is anyone who prepares for compensation all or a substantial portion of a return or refund claim. Credentials do not matter, and unpaid preparers are not covered."),
("To avoid the preparer penalty for an undisclosed position that is not a tax shelter or reportable transaction, the position needs:",
 "Substantial authority", ["Only a reasonable basis", "A realistic possibility", "Some written support"],
 "Under section 6694(a), an undisclosed position must have substantial authority. Reasonable basis is enough only when the position is adequately disclosed."),
("A preparer adequately discloses a position on the return. To avoid the unreasonable position penalty, the position needs at least:",
 "A reasonable basis", ["Substantial authority", "More likely than not support", "A private letter ruling"],
 "Adequate disclosure lowers the standard to reasonable basis under section 6694(a). Tax shelters and reportable transactions still need a more likely than not belief."),
("A paid preparer must keep a copy of each return prepared, or a list of the clients and returns, for at least:",
 "3 years", ["1 year", "7 years", "5 years"],
 "Section 6107 requires a copy or a list to be kept for 3 years after the close of the return period. Failing to do so carries a preparer penalty."),
("Which action can a state board of accountancy take against a CPA licensed in its state?",
 "Revoke the CPA's license", ["Bar the CPA from IRS practice", "Expel the CPA from the AICPA", "Impose a prison sentence"],
 "A state board issues, suspends and revokes licenses. The IRS Office of Professional Responsibility handles practice before the IRS, the AICPA handles its own membership, and only courts impose criminal sentences."),
("To recover from a CPA for common law negligence, a client must prove each of the following EXCEPT:",
 "An intent to deceive", ["A duty of care owed", "A breach of that duty", "Damages caused by the breach"],
 "Negligence needs a duty, a breach, causation and damages. Intent to deceive (scienter) is an element of fraud, not negligence."),
("The federally authorized tax practitioner privilege under section 7525 covers tax advice in:",
 "Civil tax matters before the IRS", ["Criminal tax proceedings", "Written tax shelter promotions", "State income tax audits"],
 "Section 7525 extends attorney-client style privilege to tax advice by federally authorized practitioners in noncriminal federal tax matters. It does not cover criminal cases, written tax shelter promotion or state matters."),
]
